final. Ledecky went out ahead of world record pace for 200 metres, with Adlington and Friis trying to hang on. By 600 metres she had moved two bodylengths in front and had only to hold on for the gold medal. Ledecky won in 8:14.63, the second fastest time ever, just off Adlington’s world record of 8:14.10 from the Beijing Olympics, but Ledecky’s was done in a textile suit. Adlington suffered in the final laps and dropped back to bronze, with the silver taken by Spain’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/112294"} "Mireia Belmonte"] ". Friis also faded near the end and placed fifth."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Ledecky became the youngest ever champion in this event at 15 years, 139 days, bettering the previous youngest, who was 1976 East German champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/47549"} "Petra Thümer"] ", then 15-177. It also made her the seventh youngest individual swimming gold medalist in any Olympic event, and sixth youngest woman."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/126653"} "Katie Ledecky"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "8:23.84 (1 h3)"] [:td {} "8:14.63 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/112294"} "Mireia Belmonte"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ESP"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ESP.png"}] "ESP"]] [:td {} "8:25.26 (2 h3)"] [:td {} "8:18.76 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/115710"} "Becky Adlington"]] [:td {} [:a {:hre